262:%\item Let $G$ be a finite abelian group. A character of $G$ is a homomorphism $\chi: G \rightarrow \mathbb{C}^$, where $\mathbb{C}^$ is the set of complex numbers with absolute value 1. The trivial character of $G$, denoted by $\chi_0$, is defined as $\chi_0(g)=1$ for all $g \in G$. The set of all characters of $G$ is denoted by $\widehat{G}$ and forms a group under pointwise multiplication, which is isomorphic to $G$.The order of a character $\chi$ is defined as the least positive integer $d$ such that $\chi^d=\chi_0$. If $G$ is a finite field $\mathbb{F}{q^n}$, then a character of the additive group of $\mathbb{F}{q^n}$ is called an additive character, and a character of the multiplicative group of $\mathbb{F}_{q^n}^*$ is called a multiplicative character.For further information regarding characters, primitive elements, and finite fields, we refer the reader to \cite{rudolf}.
